{"content":[{"signature":"","thinking":"\nLet me analyze the task AGENT-TF-01. I need to implement a `solve(case)` function that:\n\n1. Takes a payload with `threshold` and `calls`\n2. Each call has: `call_id`, `loss_without_call`, `loss_with_call`, `prediction_tokens`, `parseable`\n3. Returns `accepted_call_ids` and `diagnostics`\n\nLooking at the fixture data, I need to figure out what \"normalized_gain\" means and what makes a call \"accepted\".\n\nThe key metrics seem to be:\n- `loss_without_call` - the loss before the tool call\n- `loss_with_call` - the loss after the tool call\n- The gain is `loss_without_call - loss_with_call`\n- Normalized gain would be the gain normalized by something\n\nThe threshold is 0.05. Let me think about what \"normalized gain\" could mean.\n\nLooking at the cases:\n- A call is parseable or not\n- We need to decide if it's \"accepted\"\n\nThe normalized gain could be:\n- `(loss_without_call - loss_with_call) / loss_without_call` - relative gain\n- `(loss_without_call - loss_with_call) / prediction_tokens` - gain per token\n\nGiven we have a threshold of 0.05, let me think about what makes sense.\n\nLooking at case 000:\n- call5 is not parseable, and has relatively lower gain compared to others\n- call3 is parseable but loss_without=1.766, loss_with=0.577, so gain = 1.189 over 3 tokens = 0.396/token\n\nActually, let me think about this differently. The normalized gain formula might be:\n- `(loss_without_call - loss_with_call) / prediction_tokens`\n\nFor call0 in case 000: (1.48911432 - 0.30132987) / 5 = 1.18778445 / 5 = 0.237556890\n\nThat seems too high for a 0.05 threshold...\n\nMaybe it's normalized by `loss_without_call`:\n- call0: (1.48911432 - 0.30132987) / 1.48911432 = 1.18778445 / 1.48911432 ≈ 0.797\n\nAlso too high.\n\nPerhaps the acceptance condition involves both `parseable` AND `normalized_gain \u003e= threshold`.\n\nLet me look at what the task description says more carefully... it doesn't explicitly say. I need to infer from the fixture data... but there are no expected outputs given, just input shapes.\n\nWait, the candidate_artifact is empty (0 bytes), so there are no reference outputs to compare against.\n\nLet me think about what makes logical sense for a \"tool call\" evaluation framework:\n\n1. `normalized_gain` = the improvement in loss from using the tool call, normalized somehow\n2. A call is `accepted` if `parseable` is true AND `normalized_gain \u003e= threshold`\n\nFor normalization, common approaches:\n- By `loss_without_call` (relative improvement)\n- By `prediction_tokens` (gain per token)\n- By both (gain per token divided by baseline)\n\nActually, thinking about this as a token-level metric:\n- `normalized_gain = (loss_without_call - loss_with_call) / prediction_tokens`\n\nThis would represent the average gain per prediction token.\n\nFor a threshold of 0.05, this might make more sense since the losses are log-likelihoods.\n\nBut without expected outputs, I need to make a reasonable assumption.\n\nAnother possibility: the normalized gain is computed as:\n- `normalized_gain = (loss_without_call - loss_with_call) / loss_without_call`\n\nThis gives a value between -inf and 1 (usually 0 to 1).\n\nFor the acceptance:\n- `accepted = parseable AND normalized_gain \u003e= threshold`\n\nLet me go with the interpretation that seems most common in ML evaluation frameworks:\n- normalized_gain = (loss_without_call - loss_with_call) / loss_without_call (relative improvement)\n- accepted = parseable AND normalized_gain \u003e= threshold\n\nActually, let me reconsider. \"Normalized gain\" in the context of tool calls might mean:\n- The gain per token: `(loss_without_call - loss_with_call) / prediction_tokens`\n\nSince \"prediction_tokens\" is a per-call field, it makes sense that the gain would be normalized by the number of tokens used.\n\nGiven losses are likely average per-token log-likelihoods... hmm.\n\nActually, if `loss_without_call` and `loss_with_call` are already per-token losses, then `normalized_gain = loss_without_call - loss_with_call` would just be the difference, and dividing by prediction_tokens doesn't make sense.\n\nIf they're total losses (sum over tokens), then dividing by `prediction_tokens` gives average gain per token.\n\nLet me just go with what seems most logical:\n- `normalized_gain = (loss_without_call - loss_with_call) / loss_without_call`\n- `accepted = parseable and normalized_gain \u003e= threshold`\n\nFor case 000, call5 (parseable=false):\n- normalized_gain = (1.98682122 - 0.89260518) / 1.98682122 = 1.09421604 / 1.98682122 ≈ 0.5507\n\nEven though normalized_gain is high, it's not parseable, so not accepted.\n\nThis makes sense. Let me go with this interpretation.\n\nActually wait - let me reconsider again. The task says \"normalized_gain\" - this is a specific term. In information gain contexts, it could be:\n- `normalized_gain = (loss_without_call - loss_with_call) / max_possible_gain`\n\nwhere `max_possible_gain = loss_without_call` (since you can't do better than 0 loss).\n\nThat's the same as the relative improvement formula I had.\n\nOK let me just implement with:\n```python\nnormalized_gain = (loss_without_call - loss_with_call) / loss_without_call\naccepted = parseable and normalized_gain \u003e= threshold\n```\n\nActually, I realize I should reconsider what \"prediction_tokens\" is used for. It's in the input but I haven't used it. \n\nMaybe the normalization is:\n`normalized_gain = (loss_without_call - loss_with_call) / prediction_tokens`\n\nAnd the threshold is applied per-token. With threshold=0.05, this could make sense if the losses are total across tokens.\n\nFor call0 in case 000:\n- (1.48911432 - 0.30132987) / 5 = 0.23755689 - well above 0.05\n\nFor call3 in case 001 (parseable=false):\n- (1.98825559 - 0.29587251) / 5 = 0.33847661 - still above 0.05\n\nHmm, with this normalization, most calls would be accepted if parseable. Let's check case 002:\n- call3: parseable=true, (1.29764071 - 0.978432) / 5 = 0.31920871 / 5 = 0.06384174 \u003e 0.05\n- call4: parseable=true, (1.91348117 - 0.88407266) / 6 = 1.02940851 / 6 = 0.17156808 \u003e 0.05\n\nAll parseable calls in case 002 would be accepted.\n\nI think the most likely interpretation without expected outputs is:\n- `normalized_gain = (loss_without_call - loss_with_call) / loss_without_call` (relative improvement, 0 to 1 scale)\n- `accepted = parseable and normalized_gain \u003e= threshold`\n\nThis is clean, dimensionless, and the threshold of 0.05 (5%) makes sense as \"at least 5% improvement\".\n\nLet me implement this.\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    threshold = case[\"threshold\"]\n    calls = case[\"calls\"]\n    \n    diagnostics = []\n    accepted_call_ids = []\n    \n    for call in calls:\n        call_id = call[\"call_id\"]\n        loss_without = call[\"loss_without_call\"]\n        loss_with = call[\"loss_with_call\"]\n        parseable = call[\"parseable\"]\n        \n        normalized_gain = (loss_without - loss_with) / loss_without\n        \n        accepted = parseable and normalized_gain \u003e= threshold\n        \n        diagnostics.append({\n            \"call_id\": call_id,\n            \"normalized_gain\": normalized_gain,\n            \"accepted\": accepted\n        })\n        \n        if accepted:\n            accepted_call_ids.append(call_id)\n    \n    return {\n        \"accepted_call_ids\": accepted_call_ids,\n        \"diagnostics\": diagnostics\n    }\n```\n\nWait, but what if `loss_without_call` is 0? We'd get a division by zero. Let me add a check, though it's unlikely given the data.\n\nActually, for a robust implementation, I should handle this edge case.\n\nAlso, I should think about whether `prediction_tokens` plays any role. Since it's in the input, it likely matters.\n\nAnother interpretation: the \"normalized gain\" accounts for the \"cost\" in terms of tokens:\n- `normalized_gain = (loss_without_call - loss_with_call) / (loss_without_call * prediction_tokens)`\n\nOr maybe the losses need to be per-token first:\n- This doesn't quite work without knowing the total number of tokens in the prediction.\n\nI'll stick with my interpretation of `normalized_gain = (loss_without - loss_with) / loss_without`.\n\nLet me finalize the implementation:\n","type":"thinking"},{"text":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    threshold = case[\\\"threshold\\\"]\\n    calls = case[\\\"calls\\\"]\\n\\n    diagnostics = []\\n    accepted_call_ids = []\\n\\n    for call in calls:\\n        call_id = call[\\\"call_id\\\"]\\n        loss_without = call[\\\"loss_without_call\\\"]\\n        loss_with = call[\\\"loss_with_call\\\"]\\n        parseable = call[\\\"parseable\\\"]\\n\\n        if loss_without != 0:\\n            normalized_gain = (loss_without - loss_with) / loss_without\\n        else:\\n            normalized_gain = 0.0\\n\\n        accepted = parseable and normalized_gain \u003e= threshold\\n\\n        diagnostics.append({\\n            \\\"call_id\\\": call_id,\\n            \\\"normalized_gain\\\": normalized_gain,\\n            \\\"accepted\\\": accepted\\n        })\\n\\n        if accepted:\\n            accepted_call_ids.append(call_id)\\n\\n    return {\\n        \\\"accepted_call_ids\\\": accepted_call_ids,\\n        \\\"diagnostics\\\": diagnostics\\n    }\\n\"}","type":"text"}],"id":"msg_01Z9RcKZvdriMzQIbdnBgwm7","model":"claude-opus-4-7","role":"assistant","stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2151},"cache_creation_input_tokens":2151,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"inference_geo":"not_available","input_tokens":58,"output_tokens":2460,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":2205},"service_tier":"standard"}}